00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.890
These are the best s-log picture profile settings,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:22.590
it s called portra 800, it s part of my sony
film simulation recipes and today I will show

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:28.230
you exactly how to grade it in under 1 minute.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:33.980
This is a quick color grading tutorial and
presentation of the best s log picture profile,

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.589
also called as portra 800
This is a profile that i ve been working on

00:00:38.589 --> 00:00:43.760
since my first sony film simulations recipes,
which is about 2 years now, and after a lot

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.879
of shooting testing and comparing different
gammas, color modes and settings, I came to

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:52.800
the conclusion that if you are looking for
great color right out of the camera, this

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.150
is the best picture profile for slog on sony
camera.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:03.080
This profile will give you better color rendition,
less noise artifacts and amazing skin tones!

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.250
You can find it on my website it s part of
my sony film simulation plan, which costs

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:13.900
only 5$ per year, and if you ask me it s basically
free because it includes all updated 20 sony

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.640
film simulation recipes together with the
color filter settings that will give you a

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.640
film look.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:26.000
This are some shots from my last video, this
is before and after, as you can see it looks

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.420
amazing and has really natural color, even
tho being in nature we were surrounded by

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.250
a lot of green hues, you can notice how the
profile is rendering lovely skin tones and

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.480
the green cast is barely noticeable.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.370
You can find the settings to the updated profile
and all my other film simulation settings

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.440
on my website

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.380
This is how the footage looks straight out
of camera, it has gorgeous colors and if you

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:57.320
overexpose it right at about +1.5 or 1.7 you
will get the most out of this profile with

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:04.890
least amount of noise.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.050
If you ask me, in many cases it s good to
go as it is, the only thing I like to do is

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:15.730
bring down the shadows just a bit to hide
more of the noise located in the darker areas,

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:20.150
maybe decrease saturation and increase the
contrast just a bit to put focus on the right

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.379
things and let your eyes fall where it s supposed
to.

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:28.239
You can do that by activating the splines
in the curves panel, and then in the left

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:31.670
corner, select the black point and bring it
down.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:37.499
i ll also bring down the saturation to about
35, to give it a faded look

00:02:37.499 --> 00:02:42.310
This is how you make a basic grade, simple
and easy, the profile is so good it doesn

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.310
t need much post processing to look natural
and with pleasing colors.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.919
now let me show you can get the same look
as in the original video

00:02:50.919 --> 00:02:55.520
I used the Creative Film Power Grades to grade
this footage, you can find the link in the

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.519
description

00:02:57.519 --> 00:03:02.250
In the creative film power grades you can
choose from 54 classic film colors presets

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.669
and 35 Film Contrast Gammas, multiple halations
effects and afterwards you can export them

00:03:07.669 --> 00:03:09.469
as your own Custom LUTs

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:19.280
so let me show you how to use it,
apply a contrast of your choice, and then

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:26.900
manually adjust it your liking
Next browse through all the classic film colors

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.499
and apply one
One of my favourites is the Kinecolor

00:03:30.499 --> 00:03:36.290
The name is a homage to the first successful
colour motion picture process, which was called

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.290
Kinemacolor, let me know if you want to find
out more about that.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.599
Now let s adjust the halation

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:47.069
There is something I forgot to mention in
my last video.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:52.010
The creative Film power Grades includes 2
types of Halation effects, the Davinci resolve

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:56.809
plugin which has my custom settings installed
and which is included in the Node tree by

00:03:56.809 --> 00:03:59.999
default,
but this effect will work only if you have

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:01.290
a davinci resolve license

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.239
but don t sweat it, for those who don t have
a license for davinci resolve, I included

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:07.499
manual red halation effect

00:04:07.499 --> 00:04:12.489
It s in the red halation folder, and if you
followed my previous tutorial on how powerful

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:17.299
davinci resolve power grades are, you already
have them imported, if not, go check that

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:22.000
video to learn how to use power grades step
by step from scratch.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.260
You drag n drop the halation effect on the
node and you can see the effect is already

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.260
showing

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.740
now, how to edit the intensity of the effect?

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.889
Richt click, enter compound node. A compound
node is like a nest where you group a node

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:43.470
tree to make it tidier and simplify the structure.
Enter the compound node and you will see we

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.159
have a couple nodes

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:50.879
check for the color space transform node,
here you set the gamma input, for me it s

00:04:50.879 --> 00:04:55.630
slog2 and the output is Linear
Be sure to check these settings to be the

00:04:55.630 --> 00:05:00.190
same, otherwise it s not going to work
then go to the second color space transform,

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:09.680
choose input gamma Linear and output gamma
whatever you have shot it, rec709 log

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.480
now let s go to the layer node, which is this
one, and yours might look a little bit different,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.729
I have updated the red halation, but it s
not a problem, to be sure it s the same, let

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:23.000
s reset it and re make it step by step

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.120
dezactivate the splines
Now select the white point and drag it down

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.760
completely
make a node in the middle of the spline simply

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:37.159
by pressing, and now drag the white point
back let s say 25% of the way

00:05:37.159 --> 00:05:44.520
go into the blur pannel and unlink the 3 colors,
now drag the red all the way up and wait a

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.879
second to asses if it s too much, you can
also drag greena nd blue to give different

00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:51.620
hue values to the halation

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.099
and there you have it
this is how I graded the footage and I think

00:05:55.099 --> 00:05:57.780
the creative film power grades made it so
much easier

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.880
with all the presets, I color graded the footage
in a couple of minutes, job that could have

